===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To bend the top of one's body forward while [[raise|raising]] one's shoulders.
#: {{syn|en|slouch|stoop|lean}}
#: {{ux|en|Don't '''hunch''' over your computer if you want to avoid neck problems.}}
#* '''1961''', {{w|Muriel Spark}}, ''{{w|The Prime of Miss Jean Brodie}},'' New York: HarperPerenniel, 1994, Chapter{{nbsp}}5, p.{{nbsp}}156,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/primeofmissjeanb00muri_1]</sup>
#*: Sandy, you will never get anywhere by '''hunching''' over your [[putter]], hold your shoulders back and bend from the waist.
#* '''1978''', {{w|Armistead Maupin}}, ''{{w|Tales of the City}},'' New York: Ballantine Books, “… and many happy returns,” p.{{nbsp}}76,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/talesofcity00armi]</sup>
#*: She rolled over and '''hunched''' into a [[fetal position]].
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To raise (one's shoulders) (while lowering one's head or bending the top of one's body forward); to curve (one's body) forward (''sometimes followed by'' [[up]]).
#: {{ux|en|They stood outside the door '''hunching''' themselves against the rain and puffing on their cigarettes.}}
#: {{ux|en|He '''hunched''' up his shoulders and stared down at the ground.}}
#* '''1672''', {{w|Edward Ravenscroft}}, ''The Citizen Turn’d Gentleman,'' London: Thomas Dring, Act I, Scene{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}4,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A51115.0001.001]</sup>
#*: ''Danc[ing] Mast[er].'' {{...}} not too fast {{...}} keep you[r] leg[s] straight, {{...}} don’t '''hunch''' up your shoulders so;
#* '''1924''', {{w|Ford Madox Ford}}, ''{{w|Some Do Not ...}},'' New York: Grosset & Dunlap, Part 2, Chapter{{nbsp}}2,<sup>[https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20140712/html.php]</sup>
#*: If you '''hunch''' your shoulders too long against a storm your shoulders will grow bowed....
#* '''1938''', {{w|Marjorie Kinnan Rawlings}}, New York: Scribner, Chapter{{nbsp}}17,<sup>[https://www.fadedpage.com/books/20171116/html.php]</sup>
#*: He would '''hunch''' his twisted body close and put out his gentle and crooked hand and touch the fawn.
#* '''1939''', {{w|John Steinbeck}}, ''{{w|The Grapes of Wrath}},''  New York: Viking, 1958, Chapter 10, p.{{nbsp}}142,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/in.ernet.dli.2015.215897]</sup>
#*: They sat looking out at the dark, at the square of light the kitchen lantern threw on the ground outside the door, with a '''hunched''' shadow of Grampa in the middle of it.
# {{lb|en|intransitive}} To [[walk]] (somewhere) while hunching one's shoulders.
#: {{syn|en|slouch}}
#* '''1955''', {{w|J. P. Donleavy}}, ''{{w|The Ginger Man}},'' New York: Dell, Chapter{{nbsp}}2, p.{{nbsp}}9,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/gingerman00donl]</sup>
#*: {{...}} the figure '''hunched''' up the road.
#* '''1969''', {{w|Ray Bradbury}}, “The Inspired Chicken Motel” in ''{{w|I Sing the Body Electric (short story collection)|I Sing the Body Electric}},'' New York: Knopf, p.{{nbsp}}57,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/isingbodyelectri00brad]</sup>
#*: {{...}} once we had '''hunched''' in out of the sun and slunk through a cold pork-and-beans-on-bread lunch {{...}} my brother and I found a desert creek nearby and heaved rocks at each other to cool off.
#* '''1983''', {{w|Jack Vance}}, ''{{w|Suldrun's Garden|Suldrun’s Garden}},'' Spatterlight Press, 2012, Chapter{{nbsp}}18,<sup>[https://books.google.ca/books?id=1nKJwiXe_uEC&printsec=frontcover#v=onepage&q&f=false]</sup>
#*: {{...}} wheezing and grunting he '''hunched''' across the room.
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[thrust]] a [[hump]] or [[protuberance]] out of (something); to [[crook]], as the back.
#* '''1679''', {{w|John Dryden}} and {{w|Nathaniel Lee}}, ''{{w|Oedipus (Dryden play)|Oedipus}},'' London: R. Bentley and M. Magnes, Act{{nbsp}}I, p.{{nbsp}}6,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A36657.0001.001]</sup>
#*: {{...}} thou art all one errour; soul and body.
#*: The first young tryal of some unskill’d Pow’r;
#*: Rude in the making Art, and Ape of Jove.
#*: Thy crooked mind within '''hunch’d''' out thy back;
#*: And wander’d in thy limbs:
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[push]] or [[jostle]] with the [[elbow]]; to push or thrust against (someone).
#: {{syn|en|elbow|nudge}}
#* '''1667''', {{w|Roger L'Estrange|Roger L’Estrange}} (translator), ''{{w|Los Sueños|The Visions}} of {{w|Francisco de Quevedo|Dom Francisco de Quevedo Villegas}},'' London: H. Herringman, “The Sixth Vision of Hell,” pp.{{nbsp}}182-183,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/A56893.0001.001]</sup>
#*: After this, we saw a great Troop of ''Women'' upon the ''High-way to Hell,'' with their ''Bags;'' and their ''fellows,'' at their Heels, ever, and anon, '''hunching''', and Justling one Another.
#* '''1748''', {{w|Samuel Richardson}}, ''{{w|Clarissa}},'' London: for the author, Volume 2, Letter{{nbsp}}1, p.{{nbsp}}8,<sup>[http://name.umdl.umich.edu/004835420.0001.002]</sup>
#*: Hickman, a great over-grown, lank-hair’d, chubby boy, who would be '''hunch’d''' and punch’d by every-body; and go home, with his finger in his eye, and tell his mother.
#* '''1899''', {{w|Sutton E. Griggs}}, ''Imperium in Imperio,'' Chapter{{nbsp}}6,<sup>[http://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/15454/pg15454-images.html]</sup>
#*: He let his eyes scan the faces of all the white teachers, male and female, but would end up with a stare at the colored man sitting there. Finally, he '''hunched''' his seat-mate with his elbow and asked what man that was.
#* '''1974''', {{w|Maya Angelou}}, ''{{w|Gather Together in My Name}},'' New York: Bantam, 1975, Chapter{{nbsp}}12, p.{{nbsp}}40,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/gathertogetherinangerich]</sup>
#*: She '''hunched''' me and winked.
#* '''1986''', {{w|Billy Roche}}, ''Tumbling Down,'' Dublin: Wolfhound Press, 1994, Chapter{{nbsp}}6, pp.{{nbsp}}102-103,<sup>[https://archive.org/details/tumblingdown00roch]</sup>
#*: {{...}} Crunch burst through, pretending to be in {{w|Croke Park}} or somewhere, '''hunching''' me away with his shoulder and holding the ghost of other players at bay as he picked up the football.
# {{lb|en|intransitive|colloquial}} To have a hunch, or make an intuitive guess.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010|author=Max Gunther|title=The Luck Factor|passage=People who are instinctive hunchers go through some such process at every decision-making point of their lives. It is likely that children often make decisions and discern truths by '''hunching'''.}}

